[Event "Stafford Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/I6bcI5Em"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/whCVdUeM/1qG3NOtV"]

{ Today we learn the Stafford Gambit: give up a pawn for fast pieces and a pile of traps he will not see coming. }
1. e4 { His king pawn marches to e4, claiming the centre. Meet it head on: push your own pawn to e5. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Straight back in the centre, pawn for pawn — nobody has given anything away yet. } (1... c6 { A fine move, and a completely different sort of game altogether. } 2. d4 d5 { That is the Caro-Kann, solid and slow. Today we want the sharp, open kind of fight. }) 2. Nf3 { His knight hits your pawn on e5. Do not stop to defend it: bring your own king knight out instead. [%cal Yg8f6] } 2... Nf6 { The natural developing move, and it counterattacks his pawn on e4 in the very same breath. } (2... Nc6 { Just as good — it defends the pawn the normal way. But it is not the gambit we are learning today. } 3. Bb5 a6 { That is the Ruy Lopez, one of the deepest openings ever played. A story for another day. }) 3. Nxe5 { The Stafford move: his knight grabs your pawn. Do not recapture — offer your own knight on c6 instead. [%cal Yb8c6] } 3... Nc6 { Attack his knight straight back with yours. Now he has to decide what to do with it right now. } (3... d6 { The sound, safe move — it wins the pawn straight back. But that is the quiet Petrov, not our gambit. } 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 { Even, correct chess, and nobody ever falls for a single trick in it. }) (3... Nxe4 { Also perfectly good, and perfectly calm — a real option, just not today's plan. } 4. Qe2 Qe7 5. Qxe4 d6 { He grabs your knight for free right now, but your pawn on d6 already attacks the one he has left on e5 — sound chess, but not the adventure we came here for. }) 4. Nxc6 { He takes your knight. Recapture with the d pawn, not the b pawn — the difference matters a lot. [%cal Yd7c6] } 4... dxc6 { The Stafford Gambit proper. Your bishop and queen both have open roads, and the d column is yours. [%csl Gc8,Gd8] } (4... bxc6 { Just as good by the raw numbers. But it shuts your own light bishop in behind the pawn. [%csl Rc8] } 5. Nc3 Bb4 { Playable, and a much quieter kind of afternoon. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: oh no, my queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/kZFfzTvW"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ Here is the Stafford position again. He is up a pawn, but your pieces are already racing ahead of his. }
5. d3 { He props his extra pawn up with d3. Send your bishop to the square that stares straight at his weak spot. [%cal Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { Your bishop takes aim at f2 — the one square only his own king is left guarding. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Be6 { A solid enough developing square, but the bishop belongs on c5, staring straight at f2 instead. } 6. Be2 Qe7 7. f4 { A sound game, and a good deal quieter than the trap we are setting up. }) (5... Qe7 { Also good, and she eyes the open e column from there. But the bishop wants c5 first. } 6. Nc3 Be6 { Perfectly playable chess, just with no trap lying in wait for him. }) 6. Bg5 { He pins your knight to the queen with his bishop. Look again, though — is that knight really pinned? [%csl Rf6] } 6... Nxe4 { It never was a real pin. Now his bishop hangs to your knight, and so does your own queen. [%cal Ge4g5,Ge4f2] } (6... Bxf2+ { Also winning, and very tempting to grab right away. } 7. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 8. Ke1 Nxg5 { A pawn and a bishop won. Good, but the queen herself was the bigger prize. }) 7. Bxd8 { He grabs your queen with the bishop, and that walks straight into mate in two. Taking the knight with his d pawn was his better try — still lost, just not to an instant mate. Now check with your bishop. [%cal Yc5f2] } 7... Bxf2+ { Check! A pawn falls too, and his king has nowhere safe or comfortable left to run. } (7... Kxd8 { The natural recapture, and it throws away everything you have built here. } 8. dxe4 Bd6 { You would simply be a queen down for good. The check on f2 had to come first. }) (7... Nxf2 { The wrong piece takes. This one gives him time to organise instead of panic. } 8. Qh5 b6 9. Qg5 { His queen swings over to take aim at g7 instead, and your whole attack has gone missing. }) 8. Ke2 { Forced — there is nowhere else for his king to hide. Bring your last piece out and finish the job. [%cal Yc8g4] } 8... Bg4# { Checkmate! Your two bishops box his king in on e2, and your knight on e4 seals off the one escape square. } (8... Bb6 { The bishop retreats to safety instead, and the whole mating idea disappears with it. } 9. h3 Ng3+ 10. Kf3 { He ends up a full queen ahead, and you had mate in a single move sitting right there. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: oh no, my knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/qiKryhNu"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ This time he pushes his pawn past your knight instead of propping it up. Where should the knight go? }
5. e5 { His pawn chases your knight away. Jump it forward instead, right into the middle of his own camp. [%cal Yf6e4] } 5... Ne4 { Straight to e4. Your knight grabs the centre and sets a nasty trap if he is tempted to grab it back. [%csl Ge4] } (5... Nd5 { The safe, central retreat, and a completely sound choice in its own right. } 6. d4 c5 7. c3 { A perfectly sound position for you. But there is no trap here, and no fun either. }) (5... Ng8 { All the way home to g8. Safe, but two whole moves have gone to waste getting there. } 6. Bc4 Qd4 { Playable, but he simply stands better after wasting your time like that. }) 6. d3 { The natural pawn kick. Your knight on e4 is attacked — but do not move it away just yet. [%csl Re4] } 6... Bc5 { Ignore the attacked knight completely. Your bishop joins the attack on f2 instead. [%cal Gc5f2] } (6... Nc5 { The safe retreat, and he simply stands better once the knight backs away. } 7. d4 Nd7 { You saved the knight, sure, but the whole point of the whole line went with it. }) 7. dxe4 { He grabs the knight — and that is the losing choice. Trading bishops with Be3 first was the move that survives. Now check with your bishop. [%cal Yc5f2] } 7... Bxf2+ { Check! A pawn falls too, and his king is forced to step somewhere unsafe. } (7... Qxd1+ { Grabbing his queen first, and it throws the whole combination away for nothing. } 8. Kxd1 Bxf2 9. Nd2 { You end up simply a piece down, with none of the mating ideas left. }) (7... Qh4 { It looks like a check from a distance, but the pawn still sitting on f2 blocks the diagonal — it is not one. } 8. Qf3 Bd4 { He gets the time to organise his defence, and your whole attack fizzles out. }) 8. Kxf2 { His king is forced to recapture, and that leaves his own queen with nobody left guarding her. [%cal Yd8d1] } 8... Qxd1 { Oh no — his queen. You gave up a knight and a bishop along the way, but his whole queen more than makes up for both. } (8... Qh4+ { A real check this time, but it only lets his king run while his queen escapes clean. } 9. Kg1 Qxe4 10. Qe2 { Now you have given up a knight and a bishop for one pawn — a real disaster, not the trade the check on f2 gives you. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: he does not take the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/4n6UwK64"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p5/2b1P3/4n3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p5/2b1P3/4n3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_7"]

{ Same position again, but this time he spots the trap and leaves your knight alone. }
7. Be3 { His best try: offer to trade off the bishop that is glaring at f2. Take it with yours. [%cal Yc5e3] } 7... Bxe3 { Trade it off. Once his own pawn recaptures, his king loses its shield and the diagonal in front of it stands wide open. } (7... Nxf2 { A wild sacrifice, and he simply takes the knight. } 8. Kxf2 Bxe3+ 9. Kxe3 { A knight down for one pawn — the bishops only trade off evenly along the way, not the disaster it first looks like. }) 8. fxe3 { His king has lost the pawn that shielded it, and the diagonal to e1 now lies wide open. Bring your queen out with check straight away. [%cal Yd8h4] } 8... Qh4+ { Check! He has only two ways to answer — block with g3, or move his king to e2, where your bishop would mate him on the spot. [%tail] } (8... Nc5 { The knight finally retreats, and the one moment for this whole idea slips away. } 9. Qh5 Qe7 { He stands better again once the check never comes. That check was the entire point of the line. }) 9. g3 9... Nxg3 10. hxg3 10... Qxh1 { Your queen scoops up the rook sitting in the corner — a whole rook, for the single knight you handed back. } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: he grabs the centre"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/OPZzl8KT"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p5/4P3/4n3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p5/4P3/4n3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]

{ Your knight sits back on e4, just like last time. This time he tries building a big centre instead. }
6. d4 { He grabs central space instead of kicking your knight away. Now your queen has a bold square waiting. [%cal Yd8h4] } 6... Qh4 { Your queen swings out to h4 and stares straight at f2. [%cal Gh4f2] } (6... Be6 { Just as good, a calm and perfectly normal developing move. } 7. c3 f5 { Solid chess, but the queen's trip to h4 is the move that actually sets the trap. }) (6... f5 { You prop the knight up with a pawn instead. Reasonable, and a good deal slower. } 7. Be2 Be7 { He gets to castle away in peace, and your whole attack never really arrives. }) 7. g3 { He kicks your queen with the pawn, gaining a move while he is at it. Let the knight go instead of running. [%csl Rh4] } 7... Nxg3 { A knight for a single pawn, and his king side pawns are torn wide open for it. [%cal Gh4h1] } (7... Qh3 { The queen steps aside and keeps her eye on things. But nothing concrete follows from it. } 8. Bg2 Nc5 { He is perfectly fine here, and the whole attacking moment has slipped away. }) 8. fxg3 { He recaptures with the pawn. Now for the fork — and his rook in the corner drops. [%cal Yh4e4] } 8... Qe4+ { Check! Your queen forks his king down the e column and his rook on h1 along the diagonal, both at once. [%tail] } (8... Bb4+ { A check first looks clever, but it just gives him a free tempo to tidy up his position. } 9. Kf2 Qe4 10. Bg2 { He has effectively castled by hand now, and holds everything together. }) 9. Be3 9... Qxh1 { The rook falls to your queen, and the knight you sacrificed earlier has now paid for itself twice over. } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the most common trap"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/rRL32xpq"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ He develops his knight this time instead of the pawn. Your bishop still wants that very same diagonal. }
5. Nc3 { A normal developing move from him. Point your bishop at f2 again, the same square as always. [%cal Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { Your bishop lands on c5, staring straight down the diagonal through to f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Be6 { A good, solid square for the bishop, but c5 is where it actually bites into his position. } 6. d3 Qe7 { Playable chess, and completely harmless to him from over there. }) (5... Qe7 { Also fine on its own merits. But the bishop is your sharpest attacking piece in this position. } 6. d3 Be6 7. Be2 { You will want that bishop sitting on c5 sooner or later anyway. }) 6. d3 { The most natural move on the board — and the most common mistake there is. Playing h3 first, to stop the knight jump, was the move that actually holds. } 6... Ng4 { Two of your pieces hit f2 at once now. He only has one king, and he cannot guard it twice over. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... O-O { Sensible enough, and he simply stays a clean pawn ahead this way. } 7. Be2 Ng4 8. Bxg4 { He trades the knight off for his bishop, and your whole attack has nothing left to bite on. }) 7. Be3 { His only real try at defence. Take the offer with your knight, not with the bishop. [%cal Yg4e3] } 7... Nxe3 { Grab the bishop he just handed you. Taking this way also drags a pawn away from his own king. } (7... Bxe3 { The other way to capture. It looks identical, and it is nothing of the sort. } 8. fxe3 Nxe3 9. Qd2 { His queen simply scoops up your knight afterward, and he is completely fine again. }) 8. fxe3 { Now take the pawn back too. His king has lost its cover for good, and he still has no safe home for it. [%cal Yc5e3] } 8... Bxe3 { The pawn comes right back too, and your bishop sits deep in his position while his king stays stuck at home. } (8... Qg5 { Also a strong try, and worth about the same amount as the direct recapture. } 9. Qf3 Bxe3 { The very same idea arrives anyway, just landing one move later than before. }) (8... Qh4+ { A check first if you would rather, and it comes out to about the same result. } 9. g3 Qg5 { Fine either way. Taking the pawn back on e3 is simply the cleaner route home. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the knight comes back"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/BxXuPwWK"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_6"]

{ Same set-up as before: his knight is out, and your bishop on c5 is still aiming straight at f2. }
6. Bc4 { He develops his bishop aggressively instead of playing d3. The same knight jump to g4 still works. [%cal Yf6g4] } 6... Ng4 { Both your pieces hit f2 again, and this time his own bishop is hanging loose as well. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... O-O { Safe and sensible castling, and the sharp moment quietly slips away unused. } 7. f3 b5 8. Bb3 { He stays a pawn up and gets on with consolidating his position. }) (6... Bxf2+ { The greedy check. His king simply steps across, and you have handed over a whole bishop for it. } 7. Kxf2 Qd4+ 8. Ke1 { A bad trade in the end — a whole bishop given away for just one pawn. }) 7. Qf3 { His queen slides to f3, guarding f2 and eyeing f7 at once. Your knight is hanging loose — where does it go? [%csl Rf2,Rg4] } 7... Ne5 { Back it comes to e5: it hits his queen and his bishop, and guards your f7 pawn at the very same time. [%cal Ge5f3,Ge5c4,Ge5f7] } (7... O-O { Safe castling, and he only stands a little worse for it now. } 8. h3 Nf6 { The knight heads for home, and the sharp, fun part of this line is over. }) (7... Bxf2+ { Tempting, but his king simply steps across the check and nothing more comes of it. } 8. Ke2 O-O { A whole bishop given up for a single pawn — not a trade worth making. }) 8. Qe2 { His queen steps aside out of danger. Bring your own queen out now and pile onto the attack. [%cal Yd8h4] } 8... Qh4 { Queen, bishop and knight all bear down on f2, the last pawn shielding his king, and he still has no safe haven to run to. } (8... a5 { A useful little move on the queen side, and nearly as strong as the direct approach. } 9. Qf1 b5 { You still keep a real edge here, but bringing the queen straight out was faster. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the quiet moves are not safe"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/Ibwl11lm"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_6"]

{ He tucks his bishop away to a quiet square this time and thinks he is safely out of the woods. }
6. Be2 { A safe square, and no target for you to hit directly. Throw your h pawn forward, straight up that column. [%cal Yh7h5] } 6... h5 { Your rook pawn walks forward, opening a straight road down the h column for the rook behind it. [%cal Gh8h5] } (6... Qd4 { Even stronger — it plants the queen in the centre and hits both e4 and f2 at once. } 7. O-O Nxe4 8. Bf3 { Also very good chess. But this chapter wants to see him forced into playing h3 first. }) (6... Be6 { A normal developing move, and he simply stands better while you catch up on material. } 7. d3 Qd6 { Nothing much is happening, and he quietly keeps his extra pawn. }) 7. h3 { He stops your knight from jumping to g4. That move frees up a square for your queen instead. [%csl Gd4] } 7... Qd4 { Right into the centre she goes. Your queen hits e4 and f2 at once, and nothing can chase her away. [%cal Gd4e4,Gd4f2] } (7... Bd4 { The bishop wants that same central square too. But the queen is the piece that belongs there. } 8. d3 Qe7 { Solid enough, but he keeps his extra pawn either way. }) (7... h4 { Pushing the pawn on further. Far too slow here — he simply gets to develop in peace. } 8. d3 Be6 { Nothing much is happening yet, and you have used up a move for very little. }) 8. Rf1 { He guards f2 with the rook — one way to survive, though castling would have held even better. Anywhere careless — d3 or f3 — walks straight into a mate you deliver. [%csl Rf2] } 8... Nxe4 { A clean extra pawn, and it is safe — if his knight takes back, your queen simply recaptures on e4. } (8... Bb4 { Pinning his knight, but it gives him just enough room to breathe and untangle. } 9. d3 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 { He unwinds his position from there and ends up standing better. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: everyone falls for this one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/tWZBMLsI"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]

{ Back to the d3 line, with your bishop already sitting on c5. Now your whole king side joins the attack. }
6. Be2 { He hides his bishop on a quiet square. Send your rook pawn forward — this is the real trap of the chapter. [%cal Yh7h5] } 6... h5 { Most players jump the knight to g4 first here. We push the pawn instead, so that taking the knight later opens the h column for you. [%cal Gh8h5] } (6... Ng4 { A natural jump straight at f2, but it simply hangs the knight to his bishop. } 7. Bxg4 Qh4 8. Qf3 { Chasing the bishop only invites him to defend it with the queen — a clean piece down for nothing. }) (6... a6 { A slow little pawn move, and you are still a whole pawn down while playing it. } 7. c3 Qe7 { He sits comfortably here, and you have nothing at all to show for the gambit. }) 7. O-O { He castles right into it. Now the knight jumps to g4, and your h pawn is the bait that lured him there. [%cal Yf6g4] } 7... Ng4 { Your knight offers itself on g4, sitting right in front of the h column you just prised open. [%cal Gh5g4] } (7... h4 { Pushing the pawn on alone, without the knight to back it up. Far too slow a plan. } 8. c3 Be6 { He gets the time to organise his defence, and you end up worse off for it. }) 8. h3 { He asks the question of your knight. Do not answer by moving it — bring your queen into the attack instead. [%cal Yd8d6] } 8... Qd6 { Your queen, bishop, knight and rook now all point at his king at the very same time. [%cal Gd6h2] } (8... Ne5 { You save the knight this way, and the whole attack disappears along with the danger. } 9. Qd2 Be6 { You end up a pawn down with nothing at all to show for the whole gambit. }) 9. hxg4 { He takes the knight — and that is the losing choice. Pushing his own pawn to e5 was the move that actually holds. Take back with your h pawn. [%cal Yh5g4] } 9... hxg4 { The h column swings wide open now, and there is no defence left anywhere against the mate to come. [%cal Gd6h2] } (9... Bxg4 { The natural-looking recapture, and it throws the entire attack away in one move. } 10. Nd2 O-O-O 11. Nc4 { He trades pieces off from there and ends up standing much the better of the two of you. }) 10. e5 { A desperate block with the pawn. Take it — there is nothing to fear here. [%cal Yd6e5] } 10... Qxe5 { Your queen recaptures the pawn and keeps pouring forward into his position. } (10... Bxf2+ { Also completely crushing, but your queen was already halfway there by the direct road. } 11. Rxf2 Qxe5 { You land in the very same spot, just one move slower than taking with the queen. }) 11. Bh6 { He throws his bishop in the way as a last try. Take it with your rook, not any other piece. [%cal Yh8h6] } 11... Rxh6 { Your rook takes the bishop and joins the attack straight down the open h column. } (11... gxh6 { The pawn takes instead, and the whole winning attack vanishes along with it. } 12. Bxg4 Be6 13. Bxe6 { Your rook never even reaches the h column this way, and the position is just level. }) 12. Re1 { Nothing helps him now, whatever he tries. Check him on h2 and finish this. [%cal Ye5h2] } 12... Qh2+ { Check! His king is boxed right into the corner, with nowhere good left to run to. [%tail] } (12... Bxf2+ { This mates too in the end, just by a slower and more roundabout road. } 13. Kf1 Qe3 { Also completely winning. The check on h2 was simply the fastest way home. }) 13. Kf1 13... Qh1# { Checkmate. The rook on h6 and the queen have swept the whole file, and his king has nowhere left to run. } 0-1
